---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import AreaCalculator from '@components/islands/AreaCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('math', 'area-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate the area of a shape?', answer: 'It depends on the shape. A rectangle is length × width; a triangle is ½ × base × height; a circle is π × radius². Choose your shape above and enter its dimensions — the calculator applies the right formula automatically.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the area of a circle?', answer: 'Area = π × r², where r is the radius (half the diameter). For a circle with radius 5, the area is π × 25 ≈ 78.54 square units. If you only know the diameter, halve it first to get the radius.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the area of a triangle?', answer: 'The standard formula is ½ × base × height, where the height is measured perpendicular to the base. A triangle with a base of 6 and height of 4 has an area of ½ × 6 × 4 = 12. If you know the three sides instead, the triangle calculator uses Heron\'s formula.' },
  { question: 'What is the area of a rectangle?', answer: 'Length × width. A room 8 units long and 5 wide is 8 × 5 = 40 square units. A square is the special case where length and width are equal, so its area is side².' },
  { question: 'What units does the area come out in?', answer: 'Enter all measurements in the same unit; the area is in that unit squared. Select a unit label (cm, m, in, ft, yd) to match your measurements.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Calculate the area of a rectangle, square, triangle, circle, trapezoid, parallelogram or ellipse — just pick the shape and enter its dimensions." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }}>
  <AreaCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose your <strong>shape</strong> — rectangle, square, triangle, circle, trapezoid, parallelogram or ellipse.</li>
      <li>Enter the dimensions the shape asks for, all in the <strong>same unit</strong>.</li>
      <li>Read the area, which comes out in that unit <strong>squared</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick a unit label (cm, m, in, ft, yd) so the result is written as square centimetres, square feet and so on.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Area formulas for every shape</h2>
    <p>Each shape has its own formula, but they all return a result in square units. These are the seven the calculator handles:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shape</th><th>Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>length × width</td><td>8 × 5 = 40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Square</td><td>side<sup>2</sup></td><td>4<sup>2</sup> = 16</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triangle</td><td>½ × base × height</td><td>½ × 6 × 4 = 12</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Circle</td><td>π × radius<sup>2</sup></td><td>π × 5<sup>2</sup> ≈ 78.54</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trapezoid</td><td>½ × (a + b) × height</td><td>½ × (6 + 4) × 3 = 15</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Parallelogram</td><td>base × height</td><td>6 × 4 = 24</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ellipse</td><td>π × a × b</td><td>π × 5 × 3 ≈ 47.12</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For the triangle, <em>height</em> is the perpendicular distance from the base to the opposite corner, not the slanted side. For the ellipse, <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> are the two semi-axes — the distances from the centre to the edge along each direction.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a circular patio with a <strong>radius of 5</strong> metres. The area is π × radius<sup>2</sup> = π × 5<sup>2</sup> = π × 25 ≈ <strong>78.54 square metres</strong>. If you only know the diameter, halve it first — a 10-metre diameter is a 5-metre radius. A trapezoidal garden bed with parallel sides of 6 and 4 and a height of 3 works out to ½ × (6 + 4) × 3 = <strong>15 square units</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why area is always in squared units</h2>
    <p>
      Area measures a two-dimensional surface, so it multiplies one length by another and the units multiply too. Measure in metres and the area is in square metres; measure in feet and it is in square feet. This is also why you must convert to a single unit <em>before</em> calculating — mixing feet and inches in the same shape gives a meaningless number.
    </p>

    <h2>Measuring an irregular space</h2>
    <p>
      Real rooms and lots are rarely one clean shape. The reliable trick is to split an awkward outline into rectangles, triangles and circle segments, find each area separately, and add them. An L-shaped room, for instance, is just two rectangles. For a piece cut out — a circular pond in a rectangular yard — find both areas and subtract the hole from the whole.
    </p>

    <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Using diameter instead of radius</strong> in a circle. The formula needs the radius; the diameter is twice too big and quadruples the area.</li>
      <li><strong>Using a slanted side as the height</strong> of a triangle or parallelogram. Height is always measured at a right angle to the base.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixing units.</strong> Convert everything to one unit before you calculate, not after.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For a floor or room specifically, the <a href="/everyday/square-footage-calculator">square footage calculator</a> also estimates material cost. For 3D shapes, use the <a href="/math/volume-calculator">volume calculator</a>, and for a triangle from its three side lengths, the <a href="/math/triangle-calculator">triangle calculator</a> finds the area and every angle.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
